.TH DVBTX-ENGINE 8 2026-11-06 "dvbtx 0.2.0" "dvbtx manual"
.SH NAME
dvbtx-engine \- multiplexer, DVB modulator and radio output of dvbtx
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-engine
.RB [ \-v ]
.I engine.ini
.br
.B /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-engine \-\-probe
.I engine.ini
.I radio
.br
.B /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-engine \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dvbtx\-engine
is the real-time half of
.BR dvbtx (1).
It receives one MPEG\-TS program per UDP input, multiplexes the services of
each carrier at the exact transport stream rate of that carrier, codes each
carrier (DVB\-T, DVB\-T2, DVB\-C, DVB\-S, DVB\-S2), synthesises all the carriers
of a radio into one stream of complex samples and hands them to a radio plugin
(HackRF, UHD, SoapySDR or the null radio).
.PP
It is not meant to be run by hand.
The
.B dvbtx
supervisor writes its configuration file
.RI ( engine.ini ,
in the work directory, by default
.IR /run/dvbtx/engine.ini ),
starts it with pipes on its standard input, output and error, sends it commands
on standard input, reads one JSON object per line on standard output and its
log on standard error.
.B dvbtx engine\-ini
writes the file the supervisor would use and prints its path, which is the
easiest way to see a real one.
.PP
The engine asks the kernel to send it
.B SIGTERM
when its parent process dies
.RB ( PR_SET_PDEATHSIG ),
so that nothing stays on the air without the supervisor; for the same reason it
refuses to start when its parent is already process 1
.RI ( "fatal: parent already gone" ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
Debug log level, whatever
.B log_level
says in the file.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Prints
.I "dvbtx\-engine 0.2.0"
and exits with status 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-probe " engine.ini radio"
Loads the file, opens radio number
.I radio
(its
.B [radio
.IB N ]
section) with its plugin and closes it at once, without transmitting.
Exit status 0 when the radio could be opened, 1 otherwise (the reason on
standard error).
The supervisor runs this every 15 s for a radio that is missing.
.TP
.I engine.ini
Any other argument is the configuration file (the last one given wins).
Without one, a usage line is printed and the exit status is 2.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The file is a plain INI file:
.IP \(bu 2
blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
or
.B ;
are ignored;
.IP \(bu
.BI [ "kind index" ]
starts a section, for instance
.B [carrier 0]
or
.BR "[service 3]" ;
.IP \(bu
.IB key " = " value
lines belong to the last section; spaces around the key and the value are
removed; a key may repeat (only
.B table
uses that); keys the engine does not know are ignored.
.PP
Integers are decimal (a leading
.B 0
does not make them octal), or hexadecimal with
.BR 0x .
Other numbers are decimal (or
.BR 0x ).
Frequencies and rates are in hertz and samples per second.
A line that is not a section header or a
.IB key = value
pair inside a section is an error.
Radios and carriers must be numbered 0, 1, 2... in the order of the file.
Up to 16 radios, 128 carriers, 64 carriers per radio, 256 services per carrier
and 1024 inputs.
.SS [engine]
.TP
.B log_level
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.B warn
or
.BR error .
Default
.BR info .
.TP
.B stats_interval_ms
Period of the statistics lines on standard output.
Default 1000.
.TP
.B cpus
CPUs the engine's threads may run on (see
.BR "CPU AFFINITY" ).
.B auto
(default),
.BR all ,
or a list such as
.BR 1\-5 " or " 0,2,4\-7 .
.TP
.B cpu_latency_us
Processor wake-up latency requested through
.I /dev/cpu_dma_latency
while the engine runs (see
.BR "PROCESSOR LATENCY" ).
Default 0;
.B \-1
makes no request;
.B auto
adapts it to the gaps on air.
.TP
.B cpu_latency_max_us
With
.BR "cpu_latency_us = auto" :
the highest latency tried (default 100).
.TP
.B cpu_latency_step_s
With
.BR "cpu_latency_us = auto" :
seconds without gap before the next step (default 30).
.SS [input N]
One UDP input carrying one MPEG\-TS program (usually written by an ffmpeg
ingest process).
.I N
is the input id, 0 to 1023; ids may have gaps.
.TP
.B addr
Required.
[\fBudp://\fR][\fIiface\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR.
An empty host,
.B 0.0.0.0
or
.B *
receive on every address; a multicast group is joined, on the interface
.I iface
when given; a host name is resolved.
The socket asks for a 32 MiB receive buffer.
.TP
.B name
Name shown in the statistics and the log.
Default
.BR input .
.TP
.B program
program_number to keep from the stream; 0 (default) takes the first program of
the PAT.
.TP
.B buffer_ms
Fixed delay between the arrival of a packet and its ideal departure time.
Default 1000.
.TP
.B ring_pkts
Size of the ring of received packets, rounded up to a power of two, 1024 at
least.
Default 65536.
.SS [radio N]
.TP
.B driver
Radio plugin:
.BR hackrf " (default), " uhd ", " soapy " or " null .
.TP
.B args
Plugin arguments (see
.BR "RADIO PLUGINS" ).
Default empty.
.TP
.B freq
Required.
Centre (local oscillator) frequency in Hz.
.TP
.B rate
Required.
Sample rate in samples per second.
.TP
.B bandwidth
Analog filter bandwidth in Hz; 0 (default) lets the plugin choose.
.TP
.B gain
Transmit gain in dB, passed to the plugin.
Default 0.
.TP
.B amp
1 enables the HackRF front-end amplifier.
Default 0.
.TP
.B ppm
Frequency correction of the radio's reference, in parts per million.
Default 0.
.TP
.B clock
Clock source, used by the UHD plugin only:
.B internal
(default), or anything else UHD accepts, such as
.BR external " or " gpsdo .
.TP
.B buffer_ms
Host-side buffering of the plugin.
Default 50.
.TP
.B backoff_db
Level of the synthesised signal below full scale, in dB: the samples are scaled
by 10^(\-backoff_db/20) before the plugin converts them.
Default 13.
.TP
.B synth_threads
Worker threads of each filter bank of this radio, which compute several blocks
at once (the bank's own thread only gathers the symbols; the output is
bit-identical).
Default \-1: none below 30 MS/s, otherwise the number of processors minus one,
at most 3.
.SS [carrier N]
One multiplex.
Keys common to every standard:
.TP
.B standard
.BR dvbt " (default), " dvbt2 ", " dvbc ", " dvbs ", " dvbs2
or
.BR ts .
.TP
.B radio
Radio index.
Default 0.
Not used by
.BR ts .
.TP
.B offset
Frequency of the carrier relative to the radio's
.BR freq ,
in Hz.
Default 0.
For DVB\-T it must be a whole number of subcarrier spacings.
.TP
.B gain_db
Relative power of this carrier: the radio's power is shared between its
carriers in proportion to 10^(gain_db/10).
Default 0.
.TP
.B on
0 starts the carrier off the air (see
.BR "carrier set" ).
Default 1.
.TP
.B synth
Synthesis of a single-carrier or DVB\-T2 carrier:
.B auto
(default) uses the frequency-domain filter bank when the symbol rate is a simple
fraction of the radio rate, else the time-domain polyphase filter;
.B fft
requires the filter bank (an error otherwise);
.B fir
always uses the polyphase filter.
.TP
.B tsid
transport_stream_id of the PAT.
Default
.IR N +1.
.TP
.BR pat_period_ms ", " tdt_period_ms ", " tot_period_ms
Repetition of the PAT, TDT and TOT generated by the engine.
Defaults 100, 1000, 5000.
.TP
.B nit_pid
NIT PID listed in the PAT (program 0); 0 lists none.
Default 16.
.TP
.B tot_desc
File holding the local_time_offset_descriptor of the TOT.
Empty (default): no TOT.
.TP
.B monitor
Comma-separated list of
.BI udp:// host : port
(IPv4 addresses, at most 16) receiving a copy of the multiplex as it goes on
the air, 7 packets per datagram.
.TP
.BI "table = " pid , cycle_ms , path
Repeatable.
A file of concatenated binary sections, sent on
.I pid
once every
.I cycle_ms
(the NIT, SDT and EIT written by the supervisor).
The files are re-read once a second when their modification time or size
changes, and at once on
.B reload
or
.BR SIGHUP .
.PP
.B dvbt
keys:
.B mode
.RB ( 2k " or " 8k ,
default 8k),
.B constellation
.RB ( qpsk ", " 16qam ", " 64qam ,
default 64qam),
.B code_rate
(1/2, 2/3, 3/4, 5/6, 7/8; default 7/8),
.B guard
(denominator of the guard interval: 4, 8, 16 or 32; default 32),
.B bandwidth
(Hz, default 8000000),
.B cell_id
(default 0).
All the DVB\-T carriers of a radio must have the same mode, guard interval and
bandwidth, and the radio rate must be a whole multiple of their subcarrier
spacing.
.PP
.B dvbc
keys:
.B constellation
.RB ( 16qam ", " 32qam ", " 64qam ", " 128qam ", " 256qam ;
default 256qam),
.B symbol_rate
(default 6900000),
.B rolloff
(default 0.15).
.PP
.B dvbs
keys:
.B code_rate
(default 3/4),
.B symbol_rate
(default 27500000),
.B rolloff
(default 0.35).
.PP
.B dvbs2
keys:
.B modulation
.RB ( qpsk ", " 8psk ", " 16apsk ", " 32apsk ", " 64apsk ", " 128apsk ", " 256apsk ;
default 8psk),
.B code_rate
(default 3/4; the rates EN 302 307-1 defines for the modulation, no 9/10 with
short frames; or a DVB-S2X rate of EN 302 307-2, VL-SNR excepted, the
constellation variant following from it),
.B frame
.RB ( normal " or " short ,
default normal),
.B pilots
(default 1),
.B rolloff
(default 0.35; 0.25, 0.20, and the DVB-S2X 0.15, 0.10, 0.05),
.B gold_code
(0 to 262141, default 0),
.B symbol_rate
(default 27500000).
.PP
.B dvbt2
keys (single PLP):
.B bandwidth
(Hz, default 8000000),
.B fft
.RB ( 1k ", " 2k ", " 4k ", " 8k ", " 16k ", " 32k ;
default 32k),
.B extended
(default 1),
.B guard
(a fraction, default 1/128),
.B pilot
.RB ( PP1 " to " PP8 ,
default PP7),
.B constellation
.RB ( qpsk ", " 16qam ", " 64qam ", " 256qam ;
default 256qam),
.B rotation
(default 1),
.B code_rate
(default 2/3),
.B frame
.RB ( normal " or " short ,
default normal),
.B fec_blocks
(0: the most that fit),
.B data_symbols
(0: the longest frame up to 250 ms),
.B ti_blocks
(0: the fewest that fit the time interleaver memory),
.B l1_constellation
.RB ( bpsk ", " qpsk ", " 16qam ", " 64qam ;
default 64qam),
.BR cell_id " (0), " network_id " (0x3085), " t2_system_id " (0x8001),"
.B t2_frames
(0: 2),
.B frequency
(signalled in L1, Hz; 0: 729833333).
Combinations EN 302 755 forbids are refused.
An unknown value of
.BR fft ,
.B constellation
or
.B l1_constellation
is refused too (as an unknown DVB\-T
.BR mode ).
.PP
.B ts
keys (no radio: the multiplex is written out, paced by the system clock):
.B ts_rate
(required, bit/s) and
.B output
(required:
.BI udp:// address : port
with an IPv4 address, or a file path, optionally prefixed with
.BR file:// ,
truncated when opened; a FIFO is simply written).
.SS [service N]
.TP
.B carrier
Carrier index.
Default 0.
.TP
.B name
Service name (the whole rest of the line).
Default
.BR service .
.TP
.B sid
service_id (program_number in the PAT).
.TP
.B pid_base
First output PID of the service: the PMT is on
.IR pid_base ,
the elementary streams on
.IR pid_base +1
onwards, a PCR-only PID if needed on
.IR pid_base +31.
Default 0x100 +
.IR N \(mu0x20.
.TP
.B primary
Required: id of the input of the service's source.
.TP
.B backup
Id of the input played when the primary is down; \-1 (default) for none.
.TP
.B switch_timeout_ms
The primary is down when nothing arrived for that long and what was buffered
has played out.
Default 3000.
.TP
.B return_ms
The primary must have been up that long before the service leaves the backup.
Default 10000.
.TP
.B max_late_ms
A packet later than that behind its ideal departure time is dropped.
Default 600.
.TP
.B early_ms
A packet may leave that much before its ideal departure time.
Default 100.
.TP
.B pcr_max_ms
A PCR-only packet is inserted when no PCR has been sent for that long; 0 never.
Default 35.
.TP
.B pmt_period_ms
Repetition of the PMT.
Default 100.
.TP
.B priority
1 to 10 (other values become 5).
When the multiplex is full, the packet deadline of a service is moved 100 ms
earlier per step above 5 (later per step below): higher priorities are served
first.
Default 5.
.SS Example
What
.B dvbtx engine\-ini
writes for
.I configs/hackrf\-1mux.toml
of the source tree (the second service and its input left out):
.PP
.EX
# generated by dvbtx - do not edit
[engine]
log_level = info
stats_interval_ms = 1000
cpus = auto
cpu_latency_us = 0

[input 0]
name = Mire
addr = 127.0.0.1:20100
program = 0
buffer_ms = 1000
ring_pkts = 65536

[radio 0]
driver = hackrf
args =
freq = 478005580.357
rate = 18285714.285714
bandwidth = 20000000
gain = 30.0
amp = 0
ppm = 0.0
buffer_ms = 50
backoff_db = 13.0
clock = internal

[carrier 0]
standard = dvbt
tsid = 1
radio = 0
offset = 3994419.643
mode = 8k
constellation = 64qam
code_rate = 7/8
guard = 32
bandwidth = 8000000
cell_id = 0
gain_db = 0.0
monitor = udp://127.0.0.1:20900
table = 0x10,2000,/run/dvbtx/si/c0_nit.sec
table = 0x11,1000,/run/dvbtx/si/c0_sdt.sec
table = 0x12,1000,/run/dvbtx/si/c0_eit_pf.sec
table = 0x12,10000,/run/dvbtx/si/c0_eit_sch.sec
tot_desc = /run/dvbtx/si/tot_desc.bin
nit_pid = 16

[service 0]
carrier = 0
name = Mire
sid = 257
pid_base = 256
primary = 0
backup = \-1
switch_timeout_ms = 3000
return_ms = 10000
max_late_ms = 600
early_ms = 100
pcr_max_ms = 35
pmt_period_ms = 100
priority = 5
.EE
.SH CONTROL CHANNEL
Commands are read from standard input, one per line (at most 16383 bytes; a
longer line is dropped), when standard input is not a terminal.
Words are separated by spaces;
.IB key = value
arguments may come in any order, except
.BI name= text\fR,
which takes the rest of the line and must therefore come last.
Any command may carry
.BI seq= N\fR;
every command except
.B reload
and
.B quit
is answered on standard output by one JSON line:
.PP
.EX
{"type":"reply","seq":N,"ok":true}
{"type":"reply","seq":N,"ok":true,"note":"..."}
{"type":"reply","seq":N,"ok":false,"error":"..."}
.EE
.PP
with
.I seq
\-1 when none was given.
Changes to a multiplex are made by the thread that pulls it; the command waits
for it up to 2 s.
.TP
.B reload
Re-reads the section files and the TOT descriptor now (as
.BR SIGHUP ).
.TP
.B quit
Stops the engine (as
.BR SIGTERM ).
.TP
\fBinput add id=\fIN\fB addr=\fIaddr\fR [\fBprogram=\fIP\fR] [\fBbuffer_ms=\fIms\fR] [\fBring_pkts=\fIn\fR] [\fBname=\fItext\fR]
Adds an input, received at once.
.I N
is 0 to 1023 and must be free.
Defaults as in
.BR "[input N]" .
.TP
.BI "input del id=" N
Removes an input: once no service uses it any more (as primary, backup or
pending primary), it stops being received and is freed.
.TP
\fBservice add carrier=\fIC\fB sid=\fIS\fB pid_base=\fIB\fB primary=\fIID\fR [\fBbackup=\fIID\fR] [\fIoption\fB=\fIvalue\fR ...] [\fBname=\fItext\fR]
Adds a service to a running multiplex.
.I S
must be 1 to 65535,
.I B
0x20 to 0x1FC0; the options are those of
.B [service N]
.RB ( switch_timeout_ms ", " return_ms ", " max_late_ms ", " early_ms ,
.BR pcr_max_ms ", " pmt_period_ms ", " priority )
with the same defaults.
.TP
.BI "service del carrier=" C " sid=" S
Removes a service.
.TP
\fBservice set carrier=\fIC\fB sid=\fIS\fR [\fBprimary=\fIID\fR] [\fBbackup=\fIID\fR|\fB\-1\fR] [\fBname=\fItext\fR]
Changes a service.
A new primary is used as soon as it delivers: when the service is on its
primary, the old source plays until the new one has a video key frame due
(without video: data due), at most 15 s plus the new input's buffer later.
.B backup=\-1
removes the backup.
.TP
\fBcarrier set id=\fIN\fB on=0\fR|\fB1\fR
Takes a carrier off the air or puts it back.
It is still coded (its multiplex keeps its clock) but not added to the radio's
samples (a
.B ts
carrier stops writing); its radio and the other carriers go on.
When every carrier of a radio is off, the radio is closed (state
.BR idle )
and opened again when one comes back, its sample clock carried on.
.TP
\fBmonitor add\fR|\fBdel carrier=\fIN\fB addr=udp://\fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR
Adds or removes a copy of the multiplex (as the
.B monitor
key), used by the web page for previews.
.SH STATISTICS
Every
.B stats_interval_ms
the engine prints one line:
.PP
.EX
{"type":"stats","time":...,"uptime":...,"interval":...,
 "cpu_latency_us":...,"cpu_latency_auto":...,
 "inputs":[...],"radios":[...],"carriers":[...]}
.EE
.PP
.I time
is the wall clock (Unix seconds),
.I uptime
and
.I interval
in seconds.
.I cpu_latency_us
is the processor wake-up latency held (\-1: no request),
.I cpu_latency_auto
true while it adapts itself (see
.BR "PROCESSOR LATENCY" ).
Rates are in bit/s over the interval.
.TP
.B inputs[]
.BR index ", " name ", " addr ;
.B rx_rate
(received) and
.B rate
(packets of the selected program);
.BR cc_errors ,
.B discont
(PCR discontinuities), cumulative;
.B streams
(elementary streams of the program);
.B idle_ms
since the last datagram (\-1: never received);
.B slack_min_ms
(smallest margin between arrival and ideal departure over the last 2 s window);
.B pcr_interval_max_ms
(longest PCR interval since the previous line);
.B faster_than_realtime
(1 when the stream arrives far ahead of its timestamps).
.TP
.B radios[]
.BR index ", " driver ", " freq ", " rate ", " carriers ;
.B underruns
(gaps filled with zeros by the plugin, cumulative);
.B late
(gaps reported by the device: HackRF firmware FIFO shortfalls, UHD time or
sequence errors);
.B fill
(host buffer, 0 to 1);
.B cpu
(radio thread, percent of one core);
.B samples
(consumed by the radio);
.BR banks " and " bank_cpu
(filter-bank and DVB\-T2 group threads);
.B state
.RB ( on ", " off
(not found at start),
.B lost
(unplugged or failing since),
.B idle
(all its carriers off));
.B error
(the plugin's message when not
.BR on );
.B serial
(of the device opened, as its tools print it, empty when the driver gives none).
The radios are numbered 0 to n\-1 in the order of
.BR engine.ini ,
whatever their number in
.BR dvbtx.toml (5):
.B dvbtx
leaves out the radios without carrier and translates the numbers back.
.TP
.B carriers[]
.BR carrier " (index), " ts_rate " (channel capacity), " rate ,
.BR null_rate ", " si_rate ", " es_rate ;
.B clock_lead_ms
(how far ahead of the present the multiplexer is stamping packets: the emission
time of the slot being filled minus now);
.BR standard ", " radio
(\-1 for
.BR ts ),
.BR offset ", " cpu ;
.B on
(0 when stopped);
.BR services[] .
.TP
.B services[]
.BR index ", " name ", " sid ;
.B source
.RB ( primary ", " backup " or " none );
.BR streams ", " rate ;
.B dropped_late
(packets dropped as later than
.BR max_late_ms ),
.B overruns
(the multiplexer fell a whole ring behind),
.B pcr_inserted
and
.B late_max_ms
(latest packet sent), over the interval;
.B switches
(cumulative);
.BR video_margin_min_ms ", " video_margin_avg_ms ,
.BR audio_margin_min_ms ", " audio_margin_avg_ms
(decoding margin: DTS minus the PCR at emission, over the interval, present
only when such packets were sent);
.BR video_type ", " audio_types
(stream_type values);
.B disc
(discontinuities signalled over the interval);
.B inputs
(ids of the primary, the backup and a pending new primary, \-1 for none).
.SH LOG
Standard error carries
.IR "HH:MM:SS.mmm LVL message"
lines, LVL being
.BR ERR ", " WRN ", " INF " or " DBG ;
the supervisor copies them into its own log.
Plugins may write their own lines
.RI ( "hackrf: ..." ", " "uhd: ..." ).
A fatal error prints
.I "fatal: message"
and exits.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop: the radio threads finish their current write, the radios are closed.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the section files now.
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored.
.SH REAL-TIME PRIORITIES
The engine gives its time-critical threads
.B SCHED_FIFO
priorities:
.IP \(bu 2
radio threads (synthesis and writing to the plugin): 50;
.IP \(bu
carrier coding threads, filter-bank threads and their workers, DVB\-T2 group
threads and their workers: 40;
.IP \(bu
HackRF: the libusb event thread of libhackrf, 60 (plugin argument
.BR rt= );
.IP \(bu
UHD: the plugin's sender thread, 60; the threads UHD creates while the device
opens, 55.
.PP
The UDP receive thread and the main thread keep the normal policy.
Without the right to do so, the engine warns once
.RI ( "no real-time priority ... give the service LimitRTPRIO or CAP_SYS_NICE" )
and runs at normal priority; underruns are then likely under load.
The packaged systemd units set
.BR LimitRTPRIO=99 .
.PP
The threads are named, as
.B top \-H
or
.B ps \-L
show them:
.BR carrier N
(coding of carrier N),
.BR bank N
and its workers
.BR bank\-w N
(filter banks),
.BR t2\-w N
(DVB\-T2 group workers),
.BR radio N
(radio thread N in
.BR engine.ini ),
.B uhd\-send
and
.BR uhd\-async ,
.B inputs
(UDP reception).
A thread a library creates takes the name of the thread that started it: the
USB transport thread of UHD shows as
.BR radio N.
.SH CPU AFFINITY
.B cpus = auto
(default) reads
.I /proc/interrupts
for the USB host controllers
.RB ( xhci ", " ehci )
and their
.IR /proc/irq/N/effective_affinity_list ,
and keeps every thread of the engine (those libhackrf and libusb start later
included, by inheritance) off those CPUs, provided at least two CPUs are left;
otherwise it uses every CPU.
A real-time thread on the CPU that takes the USB interrupts delays the USB
softirq work and the radio's FIFO runs dry.
.B all
leaves the affinity alone; a list such as
.B 1\-5
pins the engine to those CPUs.
.SH PROCESSOR LATENCY
Deep processor C-states delay the USB controller's DMA; at high sample rates the
radio's FIFO (about 1 ms on a HackRF at 16 MS/s) runs dry.
Unless
.B cpu_latency_us
is \-1, the engine writes it to
.I /dev/cpu_dma_latency
and keeps the file open while it runs, which holds the processor to that
wake-up latency.
The default, 0, blocks every deep C-state; a value such as 100 lets the cores
sleep between two buffers and saves power (see
.BR dvbtx\-power (8)).
Writing the file needs root or
.BR CAP_SYS_ADMIN ;
on failure the engine warns and goes on.
.PP
With
.BR "cpu_latency_us = auto" ,
the engine starts at 0 and, after
.B cpu_latency_step_s
seconds without a new gap
.RB ( underruns " or " late
of any radio on the air), relaxes the latency to the next step, up to
.BR cpu_latency_max_us .
The steps sit just below the exit latency of each idle state of the machine
.RI ( /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/state*/latency ),
each one letting one more C-state in.
A new gap brings it back at once to the last step that held without gap (0 if
the gap came at that step), and the next try waits twice as long (up to 6 h);
a step that holds a whole wait is kept and the wait goes back to
.BR cpu_latency_step_s .
Every change is logged; the value held is
.B cpu_latency_us
in the statistics (\-1: none).
.SH RADIO PLUGINS
Each driver is a shared object
.BI dvbtx\-sdr\- name .so
loaded with
.BR dlopen (3)
at start, so the engine only needs the libraries of the radios actually used.
It is looked for in
.BR $DVBTX_PLUGIN_DIR ,
then in the directory of the
.B dvbtx\-engine
binary, then in the directory compiled in
.RI ( /usr/lib/dvbtx ).
A missing or incompatible plugin is a fatal error; a radio that cannot be
opened is not (its carriers stay off the air, state
.BR off ).
A radio that stops taking samples for 5 s (after the first 10 s) or whose write
fails is marked
.B lost
and its carriers go off the air; the other radios go on.
.PP
Plugin arguments
.RB ( args )
are comma-separated
.IB key = value
pairs.
.SS hackrf
HackRF One through libhackrf, 8-bit samples.
.B gain
is the TX VGA gain, clamped to 0\(en47 dB;
.B amp
switches the +14 dB amplifier;
.B ppm
corrects both the sample rate and the frequency;
.B bandwidth
0 means the baseband filter libhackrf computes for the sample rate (the
supervisor writes one chosen from the band edges).
The ring buffer holds
.B buffer_ms
of samples (40 ms when 0), rounded up to 256 KiB, 1 MiB at least; the radio
starts once it has been filled.
.TP
.BI serial= S
Open the HackRF with that serial number (default: the first one).
.TP
.BI if= Hz
Below 2150 MHz, force the intermediate frequency of the MAX2837 (2150 to 2750
MHz; the mixer LO is put on a whole MHz).
Default: the firmware's choice.
.TP
.BI m0_poll= s
How often the firmware's FIFO shortfall counter is read (reported as
.BR late ),
in seconds; default 1, 0 never.
.TP
.BI rt= N
SCHED_FIFO priority of the libusb thread; default 60, 0 none.
.TP
.BI cb_log_ms= N
Log a USB callback that comes more than
.I N
ms after the previous one, and the firmware shortfalls; default 0 (off).
.SS uhd
Ettus USRP (B200, B210...) through the UHD C API, one TX channel, samples sent
as
.BR sc16 .
.B args
is the UHD device string, to which the plugin adds
.BI master_clock_rate= R
(the largest whole multiple of the sample rate not above 56 MHz, or the rate
itself) and
.B num_send_frames=64
unless given.
The sample rate the device gives must be exact (otherwise set
.B master_clock_rate
yourself).
The antenna is
.B TX/RX
unless
.B antenna=
appears in
.BR args .
.B gain
is the UHD TX gain;
.B bandwidth
0 means min(56 MHz, rate);
.B clock
other than
.B internal
selects the clock source;
.B ppm
corrects both the sample rate (and the master clock it computes) and the frequency.
A ring of
.B buffer_ms
(50 ms when 0) is drained by a sender thread; underflows come from the
device's asynchronous messages.
UHD reads
.I ~/.config/uhd.conf
and needs
.BR HOME .
.SS soapy
Any SoapySDR transmitter (LimeSDR, PlutoSDR, bladeRF...), channel 0, CF32
samples.
.B args
is the Soapy device string, for instance
.B driver=lime
or
.BR driver=plutosdr,hostname=192.168.2.1 .
The sample rate given must be within 10 ppm of the one asked;
.B gain
is the overall TX gain;
.B bandwidth
is set when not 0;
.B ppm
corrects both the sample rate and the frequency;
.B amp
and
.B clock
are ignored.
Built, not tested on hardware.
.SS null
No hardware: consumes the samples at the nominal rate of the system clock
(30 ms ahead), for tests.
.TP
.BI file= path
Write the I/Q samples to
.I path
(after the backoff scaling).
.TP
.BR format=cf32 | cs8 | cs16
Sample format of that file: 32-bit float (default), signed 8-bit or signed
16-bit I/Q.
.TP
.BI absent= path
The radio is missing while
.I path
exists: opening fails, and a running radio stops (as if unplugged) once the
file appears.
Used to test the missing-radio handling.
.TP
.BI gaps= path
The radio's
.B late
counter grows while
.I path
exists (gaps on air), to test
.BR "cpu_latency_us = auto" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Normal stop
.RB ( quit ,
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGINT );
.B \-\-version
and
.B \-\-probe
with the radio found.
.TP
1
Fatal error: unreadable or invalid configuration, input that cannot be bound,
plugin not found, threads that cannot be started, parent already gone;
.B \-\-probe
with the radio not found.
.TP
2
Usage error (no configuration file).
.PP
When a radio was lost, the engine leaves with
.BR _exit (2)
and status 0 without closing that radio: its driver may hang.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DVBTX_PLUGIN_DIR
First directory searched for the radio plugins.
.TP
.B HOME
Used by UHD for its configuration file.
.PP
The supervisor finds the engine binary through the
.B engine.binary
setting, then
.BR DVBTX_ENGINE ,
then
.I engine/build/dvbtx\-engine
next to its source tree, then
.IR /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-engine .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-engine
The engine.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/dvbtx/dvbtx\-sdr\-*.so
Radio plugins
.RB ( null
always; the others when their library was found at build time).
.TP
.I /run/dvbtx/engine.ini
Configuration written by the supervisor (work directory).
.TP
.I /run/dvbtx/si/
NIT, SDT, EIT section files
.RI ( c N _nit.sec ", " c N _sdt.sec ", " c N _eit_pf.sec ", " c N _eit_sch.sec )
and
.I tot_desc.bin
written by the supervisor.
.TP
.I /dev/cpu_dma_latency
Processor latency request.
